# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-windows-cscript-tests-decode-utf8 — the native cscript tests crash on a French Windows (UTF-8 decoding error, octet 138) and feed cscript unexpanded plug-in slots
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-windows-ssh-appdata-defaults
#+DATE: 2026-11-15

* Symptom

=make test= on pfenny (French Windows, over ssh), once the build got past
=windows-ssh-build-lacks-appdata=:

#+begin_example
OWNED-COM-CLOSE-WINDOWS-MOCKED-LIFECYCLE in AUTOLISP-FRONT-END-SUITE
  Unexpected Error: #<SB-INT:STREAM-DECODING-ERROR>
  :UTF-8 stream decoding error on #<SB-SYS:FD-STREAM for "file C:\\msys64\\tmp\\tmpRF3QLDNC.tmp">:
  the octet sequence #(138) cannot be decoded.
#+end_example

* Cause

The three Windows-only tests that run =cscript= capture its output with
=uiop:run-program ... :output :string= and the default external format,
UTF-8. cscript writes in the console codepage -- cp850 on this machine, where
octet 138 (0x8A) is =è= -- so any accented message (a French error text)
makes UIOP signal while reading the capture, and the test dies without
showing what cscript said. The production code already decodes console
output as Latin-1 for exactly this reason (=%run-capturing-text=,
alfe-autocad-progid-registry-probe-fails); the tests did not.

* Fix

=owned-com-close-windows-mocked-lifecycle=,
=owned-com-ready-windows-mocked-handshake= and the USERPROFILE cscript test
pass =:external-format :latin-1=, a total decoder, and now print cscript's
stdout/stderr when its exit code is not 0, so whatever cscript complained
about is visible.

* What cscript then said (2.4.14)

With its output readable, the next pfenny run showed the real failure:

#+begin_example
close-fixture.vbs(134, 1) Erreur de compilation Microsoft VBScript: Caractère incorrect
#+end_example

Line 134 is =${PLUGIN_BEFORE_CLOSE}=. Since the plug-in system (alfe 2.2.76)
the bridge templates carry plug-in slot lines that the emitter expands with
=expand-plugin-slots=; the test wrote the raw templates, so cscript stopped
at the =$= and the bridge never answered. The test now expands the slots
(no plug-in active: the lines are removed, as in a real run) and asserts no
=${= placeholder reaches cscript. Verified on pfenny: 16/16 and 6/6 checks
for the two cscript tests.

* Resolved

clautolisp / alfe 2.4.12 and 2.4.14 (2026-11-15). The decoding crash is gone by
construction; whether cscript itself succeeds over ssh on pfenny is shown by
the next run there (the failure message now carries its output).
